<languages />
<translate>
<!--T:1-->
== Sent ==
* <code>Create</code> with a <code>Note</code> when an item appears.
* <code>Update</code> when an item that was sent as <code>Create</code> changes.
* <code>Delete</code> when an item that was sent as <code>Create</code> goes away, for example when the page is deleted or the section removed.
* Nothing for items that were never sent.

<!--T:2-->
The <code>Note</code> carries text as plain HTML and a link to the item, never wikitext. For <code>category</code> the text is the page's first paragraph. For <code>section</code> it is the section itself, because a heading is often just a time and says little.

<!--T:3-->
A feed with no followers sends nothing. Delivery goes to a server's <code>sharedInbox</code> when it has one, so a server with many followers gets one request.

<!--T:6-->
== Notes ==
Each item is a <code>Note</code> at <code>&lt;actor id&gt;/notes/&lt;item&gt;</code>, which answers while the item exists and is a 404 once it does not. For <code>category</code> the item is the page id, and for <code>section</code> it is the item's heading path and number in unpadded base64url, which stays one path segment whatever the headings hold. <code>attributedTo</code> is the actor, <code>url</code> is the page, <code>published</code> is the page's first revision, and the note is addressed to the public. The page's URL, in <code>url</code> and in the content's link, carries <code>utm_source=fediverse</code>.

<!--T:14-->
A <code>section</code> note's content starts with the heading path in bold, such as <strong>19 October 14:10</strong>. The section follows with its subsections: paragraphs and lists, links to other sites, and subheadings as bold lines. Past 500 characters of text it is cut with an ellipsis, and a link to the page ends the note. The <code>url</code>, which the link uses too, is the permanent link to the revision the section appeared in, with <code>utm_source=fediverse</code> and then that revision's anchor for the section. <code>published</code> is when the section appeared. The note has no <code>summary</code>, which Mastodon would show as a content warning. It answers while the page's current revision has the path as many times as the item's number. When a path occurs more than once, the item numbered n is the occurrence that appeared n-th, counting up the page when the page adds sections above old ones.

<!--T:13-->
A <code>category</code> item stays a note after its page leaves the category. A follower got the item as news that a page appeared in the category, and like an RSS item it should not vanish later. A page usually leaves a category because the categories were tidied or moved, which followers need not hear about. Pages a follower would not want, such as spam or mistakes, are usually deleted, and a deletion sends <code>Delete</code>. Hiding the note or sending <code>Delete</code> on removal would be permanent or would flip-flop, because a page added back later is not a new item.

<!--T:7-->
Only a feed whose page sets a [[<tvar name="1">Special:MyLanguage/Accounts#Display name</tvar>|display name]] publishes notes. Any other feed's actor still answers, with an empty outbox and no notes, so maintenance categories publish nothing.

<!--T:8-->
A page that is an item of such a <code>category</code> feed links its note from its HTML head with <code>&lt;link rel="alternate" type="application/activity+json"&gt;</code>, so pasting the page's URL into a Mastodon search finds the note. When several such feeds hold the page, the first of its categories is used.

<!--T:9-->
== Outbox ==
The outbox is an <code>OrderedCollection</code> of <code>Create</code> activities for the newest 20 items, newest first. An item is recorded when it appears, whether or not the feed has followers, and is ordered by that time.

<!--T:4-->
== Received ==
* <code>Follow</code> is accepted and stored against the feed it names. <code>Undo</code> of a <code>Follow</code> removes it.
* <code>Like</code>, <code>Announce</code> and their <code>Undo</code> are accepted and dropped.
* Replies, meaning a <code>Create</code> whose <code>inReplyTo</code> is one of our notes, are accepted and dropped.
* A <code>Delete</code> for an actor that follows nothing is dropped before its signature is checked.

<!--T:5-->
Every other request is verified by HTTP signature. Remote keys are cached, because without a cache each request holds a PHP worker while it fetches a key from another server.

<!--T:12-->
Each actor's inbox and the shared inbox take the same activities. They answer 202 to what they accept and to what they drop alike, and 401 when the signature does not verify or the activity's <code>actor</code> is not the signer. An accepted <code>Follow</code> is answered with an <code>Accept</code> sent to the follower's shared inbox, or else its inbox. The <code>followers</code> collection shows only <code>totalItems</code>, so who follows a feed stays private.

<!--T:10-->
== Signatures ==
Requests are signed and verified as Mastodon does, with [<tvar name="1">https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-cavage-http-signatures-12</tvar> draft-cavage-http-signatures-12]:
* The algorithm is <code>rsa-sha256</code>; <code>hs2019</code> is accepted as the same.
* The signed headers include <code>(request-target)</code>, <code>host</code> and <code>date</code>, and for a <code>POST</code> also <code>digest</code>, which must match the SHA-256 of the body.
* A <code>Date</code> more than an hour ahead or twelve hours behind is refused.
* The <code>keyId</code> is the URL of a remote actor's <code>publicKey</code>. The key is fetched from it with <code>Accept: application/activity+json</code> and cached for a day. A signature that fails against a cached key is checked once more against a fresh one, since the remote may have changed its key.

<!--T:11-->
The wiki's own key pair is generated the first time it is needed and stored in the database, so nobody has a secret to keep. The public key every actor publishes is derived from the stored private key.
